1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:11,759
In the previous episode we learned how to
remember values with types. A student can
have an age, a name, a percentage, a pass
flag. That solves storage. It does not yet
solve work.

2
00:00:11,879 --> 00:00:21,239
Suppose the gradebook already holds marks
and passingScore. We need to know whether
the student passed, compute a weighted
total, maybe bump a counter.

3
00:00:21,359 --> 00:00:34,640
We are no longer asking "what is this
value?" We are asking "what do we do with
these values together?" That is where
operators arrive. An operator is a compact
way to combine, compare, or transform
values.

4
00:00:34,759 --> 00:00:44,119
The danger is not that operators are hard
to spell. The danger is that a single
character can change the meaning of a
whole expression — and the code can still
compile.

5
00:00:44,239 --> 00:00:53,279
Before lists of symbols, learn the rules
that actually decide outcomes: precedence
and associativity. Precedence answers
which operator binds first.

6
00:00:53,399 --> 00:00:58,599
Associativity answers how equal-precedence
operators group when they sit side by
side.

7
00:00:58,719 --> 00:01:08,359
Memorizing every trivia table is less
useful than knowing when to add
parentheses because the default reading is
not the reading you meant. Look at this
java for a moment.

8
00:01:08,479 --> 00:01:24,759
10 + 2 * 5 is twenty because
multiplication binds tighter than
addition. If you wanted sixty, you needed
parentheses. x >= 0 && x < 100 is a common
range check; the comparisons happen, then
&& combines the booleans.

9
00:01:24,879 --> 00:01:36,519
Parentheses are how you document intent
for the next reader, including future you.
Assignment versus equality is another one-
character fork. = stores. == compares.

10
00:01:36,640 --> 00:01:45,480
Mixing them in a condition is rarer in
modern Java than in C — but the mental
mix-up still shows up when people read
code aloud and say "equals" for both.

11
00:01:45,599 --> 00:02:00,119
Once arithmetic and comparison feel
familiar, side effects show up —
especially ++ and --. Look at this java
for a moment. Walk it carefully. x++
yields the current value of x, then
increments.

12
00:02:00,239 --> 00:02:15,680
++x increments first, then yields the new
value. Starting at five, the left side
contributes five and leaves x at six; the
right side bumps x to seven and
contributes seven; y becomes twelve. The
expression is legal.

13
00:02:15,799 --> 00:02:30,199
It is also a terrible place to hide
business logic. Prefer clarity over clever
increments. Another operator trap looks
quieter: boolean operators that short-
circuit versus ones that do not. && stops
early.

14
00:02:30,319 --> 00:02:42,959
If the left side is false, the right side
never runs. & on booleans evaluates both
sides. That difference matters when the
right side has a cost — or a crash. Look
at this java for a moment.

15
00:02:43,079 --> 00:02:52,639
With &, both sides run, so name.length()
executes even when name is null. With &&,
the null check protects the second call.

16
00:02:52,759 --> 00:02:59,719
The same short-circuit idea applies to ||:
if the left side is already true, the
right side is skipped.

17
00:02:59,840 --> 00:03:10,599
Then comes the comparison question that
follows from Episode Four's primitive-
versus-reference split: when you write ==,
what are you comparing? For primitives, ==
compares values.

18
00:03:10,719 --> 00:03:20,719
For objects, == compares identity —
whether two references point at the same
object. Content equality usually means
equals. Look at this java for a moment.

19
00:03:20,840 --> 00:03:31,640
If you accidentally use == for string
content, you may get true in a demo
because of interning, then false in
production when the strings were built
differently. Prefer equals when you mean
content.

20
00:03:31,759 --> 00:03:43,560
Save == for identity, null checks, and
enums where identity is the point. You can
recite an operator table and still ship a
bug the first time precedence, short-
circuiting, and == collide in one
condition.

21
00:03:43,679 --> 00:03:56,599
The durable skill is slower: read an
expression the way the compiler does, then
rewrite it until a human can too. (Bitwise
operators — masks and packed flags — exist
for another job entirely.

22
00:03:56,719 --> 00:04:09,279
You will meet them when a problem actually
needs bits. Today, do not confuse them
with boolean && and ||.) Variables gave us
named values. Operators let us compute and
decide with those values.

23
00:04:09,399 --> 00:04:23,359
But once you can produce a boolean, a new
pressure appears. Knowing that passed is
true is not enough. The program must take
different paths — print a pass message,
retry a download, loop through every
student.

24
00:04:23,479 --> 00:04:29,279
Computation alone does not choose a route.
That pressure is what control flow exists
to answer.
